                ],
                [
                    207.0,
                    1202.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "H.1.5.4 Disadvantages of cross certification",
            "block_bbox": [
                137,
                1221,
                593,
                1248
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    137.0,
                    1221.0
                ],
                [
                    593.0,
                    1221.0
                ],
                [
                    593.0,
                    1248.0
                ],
                [
                    137.0,
                    1248.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "While cross certification helps for flexible management of trust relationships, organizations need to be aware of the following issues regarding cross certificates.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                1271,
                1119,
                1322
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    1271.0
                ],
                [
                    1119.0,
                    1271.0
                ],
                [
                    1119.0,
                    1322.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    1322.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "If organization A cross certifies organization B's certificate(s), the security level of B's PKI will be affected by A as PKI-B should fulfill the required security levels of PKI-A because the validator that trusts A will go through the certificates of PKI-B having an illusion that these certificates are issued by PKI-A. If the certificate chain of PKI-B is compromised, and hence should not be trusted, the validator (e.g. EV) that trusts PKI-A can falsely trust the chain because the chain is apparently signed by the PKI-A. Therefore, before cross-certification, A makes sure B's PKI policy is compatible with A's PKI. This requirement may complicate the policy management of the PKIs involving cross-certification.",
            "block_bbox": [
                210,
                1323,
                1123,
                1532
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
